      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Lords exercised legal control over key economic resources and local markets, imposing mandatory fees (banalities) and often setting prices, which restricted peasants’ ability to trade freely.",
      "evidence": "Charters granting lords market rights; records of mill, oven, and bakehouse fees; studies of banalities (e.g., Bloch, *Feudal Society*, 1961).",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Statutory interventions such as the Statute of Labourers (1351) capped wages and limited peasant mobility, demonstrating a systemic effort to keep labor cheap and extract surplus.",
      "evidence": "Text of the Statute of Labourers and its enforcement after the Black Death; analyses in economic histories of medieval England.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Isolated instances of relatively benevolent lords existed, but they were rare and did not constitute a systematic practice of fair trade.",
      "evidence": "Barbara A. Hanawalt, *The Ties that Bound* (1998), notes occasional lenient lords but emphasizes that the overall manorial economy remained coercive and extractive.",
